#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161846 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "6276f196-80de-58b7-acb6-a715ee955f6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9073" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/50/501074b2c8aa951c9236dc628de2a564f31b21b5ac5b4dd34d7b1324d4c8a331.svg)\nIf $Y =1-3X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X,$ where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = 1-3X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $0 < x < 1,$ we have $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1 .$\n\n![](images/3b/3bb641b1920d0ded33e4ffa25f3e0af856550dd3820edf30416bd79c3a6da264.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(1-3X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-3X\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge \\frac{1-y}{3}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(\\frac{1-y}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{1-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1-y}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot (\\frac{1-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(1-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n![](images/b0/b00d02901c82c0544e8e576577832184919da2338cf05d2d8a3fa230d45993c4.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161847 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f1f64d-e88e-5f60-8655-459b15eaf715" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b9f862ab-0544-503e-8627-3de73ec60594" :question/math-academy-id "e-9074" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/33/3357fbadc12363114c7274b17df9f8e992566d53f8233841783338687b88989e.svg)\nIf $Y = (2-X)^3,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X,$ where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = (2-X)^3$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0 \\lt x \\lt 2,$ we have $0 \\lt y \\lt 8 .$\n\n![](images/2c/2c07ffe5af282ba1689cb6f49201b979afb4186d0128dd6a97f446b7240d76d6.png)\n\n \n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P((2-X)^{3}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2-X\\le {y}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =P(-X\\le {y}^{1/3}-2) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge 2-{y}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(2-{y}^{1/3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(2-{y}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(2-{y}^{1/3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(2-{y}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(2-(2-{y}^{1/3}))^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(2-2+{y}^{1/3})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}({y}^{1/3})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n![](images/d8/d858994c802b37d5867c0b8940848fec484bf42c48ecb525c5d518165ade60c6.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7fa984-dcc6-51d3-a178-09d0e7e701a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551699} {:answer/id #uuid "39e8db46-65a2-5088-a0fc-7d008a801433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551803} {:answer/id #uuid "600d0ed3-d6f7-5bed-9243-c5cd652cb251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551897} {:answer/id #uuid "69593d18-dc90-550d-8f2b-fef2e3f9c883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551910} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7be294-62d9-5518-9eb0-0262367daae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-8y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69593d18-dc90-550d-8f2b-fef2e3f9c883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551910} :answer-field/id #uuid "64902e3c-53ad-5d5a-a122-1bd9b485f300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80402a14-4dfc-5a15-8269-ddd0f957785c" :question/math-academy-id "q-108501" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x},\\, & x>0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x>0,$ we have $y>0.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b2d1487d180db1df306cd8c83088ef0334d29769bd7ef94eed7f75d4e6cedf7.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{-4\\cdot y/2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46564b22-49a3-5dd7-851e-0ef24aa530e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{(1+y)}^{2},\\, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551837} {:answer/id #uuid "472e5251-de8e-5c8f-906b-4486590c0f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2(1-y),\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551839} {:answer/id #uuid "53f81be5-441e-51b6-84d8-52f835d28bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551866} {:answer/id #uuid "6f344b2d-2e68-5f7d-a74f-c89cb5cf5253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2},\\, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551928} {:answer/id #uuid "8a45c7a0-ff71-52fb-9d02-51c4c15a7b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{(1-y)}^{2},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a45c7a0-ff71-52fb-9d02-51c4c15a7b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{(1-y)}^{2},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552003} :answer-field/id #uuid "5108732e-f4b1-55bf-ab3d-9aaa670d4ed1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37fc4f1c-699a-5034-b164-6cf41c6e46b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110106" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=1-\\frac{1}{3}X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=1-\\frac{1}{3}X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $0<x<3,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/af/afe10720ce51bc3dd1b304ea276568d0e505f90159359f5ae54462e4caa729e7.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(1-\\frac{X}{3}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-X\\le 3y-3) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge 3-3y) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 3-3y) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(3-3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-3)\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(3-3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3-3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3-3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}(3-3y)^{2} \\\\  & =3{(1-y)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{(1-y)}^{2},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533ff78c-a4d2-572e-aa52-f826f311b683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.02y, & 0<y<10 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551860} {:answer/id #uuid "580d8eab-cee9-52ff-8594-cc7c4cf9f774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-200y, & -0.1<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551875} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5095e2-f6a2-5b2f-833a-1ffb143c4f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552219} {:answer/id #uuid "f445b8e1-6995-53c8-b3fe-7a9a2f3140f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.002{y}^{2}, & -10<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552237} {:answer/id #uuid "f894221d-2427-5570-8811-51ea50f7082e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-0.02y, & -10<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f894221d-2427-5570-8811-51ea50f7082e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-0.02y, & -10<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552244} :answer-field/id #uuid "a19812d1-1d0a-5303-9a54-a3aaf278c49f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09cc9808-be42-54c9-b4a2-69b0d9a20cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-110111" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x,\\, & -1<x<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=10X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=10X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $-1<x<0,$ we have $-10<y<0.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b03f4d44db5b0db0498469f80fa25e56177a8602c657d8506105837212bf5ba.png)\n\n*Note: Image not drawn to scale.*\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $-10<y<0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(10X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 0.1y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(0.1y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $-10<y<0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0.1\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(0.1y) \\\\  & =0.1\\cdot {f}_{X}(0.1y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =0.1\\cdot {f}_{X}(0.1y) \\\\  & =0.1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0.1y \\\\  & =-0.02y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-0.02y, & -10<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f1f64d-e88e-5f60-8655-459b15eaf715" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100bdc8b-84b9-522b-95a8-62a7c19d7ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt[4]{y}},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551701} {:answer/id #uuid "1da9150a-4f27-53df-84d6-afffbc13c04b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt{{y}^{3}},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551728} {:answer/id #uuid "513b7cbf-3854-54d3-8df7-b61ae060993c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551856} {:answer/id #uuid "e1de8cd4-39ab-557a-bd23-6dbdab09b64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552188} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1549c6-a005-585d-aaa9-47cb105e6439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee1549c6-a005-585d-aaa9-47cb105e6439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552220} :answer-field/id #uuid "38e37aa3-43fc-561c-9a91-d1b0e6848656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "670a140c-55d4-5114-b612-9d3a283512c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-110124" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={X}^{4},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note  that since the possible values of $X$ are $0<x<1,$ $Y={X}^{4}$ is a strictly increasing function of $X$ when $0<x<1.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/9a/9a6f01a3538711e45e581038af5eaf95e229bf72d08bafefbcf34786a40c1d2d.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{4}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le {y}^{1/4}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}({y}^{1/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{y}^{3/4}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}({y}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{y}^{3/4}}\\cdot {f}_{X}({y}^{1/4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{4{y}^{3/4}}\\cdot {f}_{X}({y}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{y}^{3/4}}\\cdot 2({y}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f1f64d-e88e-5f60-8655-459b15eaf715" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "196dbf90-694f-56e1-bd9a-bacffff31ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551718} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca12552-8ad4-5d6a-8884-8f4afde5830a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5,\\, & 0<y<0.2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551849} {:answer/id #uuid "52cef4e5-3e19-5e35-bee0-384a657d1c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.5y,\\, & 0<y<2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551858} {:answer/id #uuid "a623ad96-993c-5f91-994d-d43006007fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552064} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e7845f-1c6b-5825-bbe7-1626dc1077e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.5,\\, & 0<y<2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "196dbf90-694f-56e1-bd9a-bacffff31ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551718} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bffd42c-4c16-5c26-a4d3-ff06e8bcf1b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f799a56-7472-58ef-998d-84e22fb88853" :question/math-academy-id "q-110126" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4},\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=1-{X}^{5},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=1-{X}^{5}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/05/05534475aecc1d0ebad2234f1259d1b6fd1c6183e47ad763f17c87c5fdb06afb.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(1-{X}^{5}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-{X}^{5}\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge (1-y)^{1/5}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}((1-y)^{1/5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{1}{5(1-y)^{4/5}})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}((1-y)^{1/5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5(1-y)^{4/5}}\\cdot {f}_{X}((1-y)^{1/5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{5(1-y)^{4/5}}\\cdot {f}_{X}((1-y)^{1/5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5(1-y)^{4/5}}\\cdot 5{((1-y)^{1/5})}^{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f1f64d-e88e-5f60-8655-459b15eaf715" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f11dd5-3815-523d-8b4f-218a0c887321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551865} {:answer/id #uuid "76d37b1f-0cae-52e4-bbc9-ab6fd6b7061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y},\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551946} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5a5e16-2b43-5e40-8cb3-9fe6c9bc2a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551954} {:answer/id #uuid "e933345b-51e3-5f41-b177-21de967b42a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552210} {:answer/id #uuid "ee37ca3b-30b3-5807-a624-f62ac29989e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53f11dd5-3815-523d-8b4f-218a0c887321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551865} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea541e01-5fa9-5c1a-9bd5-70920b6a9e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3a2a8f5-8f7b-5578-9df3-f4e28dce2d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-110130" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x>1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that the possible values of $X$ are $x\\in (1,\\infty ),$ and $Y=\\frac{1}{X}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$ when $X>1.$\n Moreover, for $x>1,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/16/16f3dc78752c8cfcb6b6968e3d57bb3639a4205e2a32dfd569c8c2ca3c29a589.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{1}{X}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge \\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f1f64d-e88e-5f60-8655-459b15eaf715" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cf00d4-bb82-5dd8-a677-72fc28f792e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551669} {:answer/id #uuid "3137f17a-3a0e-5b0c-a694-056680cb7764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551782} {:answer/id #uuid "59577974-15fa-52fa-8f57-a46673040992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2y}\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551879} {:answer/id #uuid "7105b7b3-58d8-53b8-a82e-927f96c5336f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551932} {:answer/id #uuid "98739189-0bce-58a6-b3ce-c59b0a5d2aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98739189-0bce-58a6-b3ce-c59b0a5d2aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552035} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a17ede-9d12-55e8-9a15-7ee9a3ba31af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a4b19843-0372-523c-be3f-e38cf328fccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-110245" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that since the values of $X$ are $0<x<1,$ $Y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $y>0.$\n\n![](images/5a/5a2a6f4c5e26819af509e9347fb7aff230927bdd9192ad10a48ce9c951c7be80.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡X\\ge -y) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge {e}^{-y}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}({e}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-{e}^{-y})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}({e}^{-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot {f}_{X}({e}^{-y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot {f}_{X}({e}^{-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f1f64d-e88e-5f60-8655-459b15eaf715" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under a Nonlinear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "679eafa7-4671-5f81-9026-857bfb15b568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{4y},\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551907} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4a131d-65cf-5e71-9eb3-dac170dbbf51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{e}^{12y},\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552014} {:answer/id #uuid "acfa5a4c-add0-565a-9ded-15a152272ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{y}^{2}{e}^{-4{y}^{3}},\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552080} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9720cc-9e97-5b98-9dc1-bae43a45708b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{y}^{2}{e}^{4{y}^{3}},\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552156} {:answer/id #uuid "f63e4602-5159-57f7-952a-0152cc8abc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{e}^{-12y},\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce9720cc-9e97-5b98-9dc1-bae43a45708b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{y}^{2}{e}^{4{y}^{3}},\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552156} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d1d0c4-c915-5b43-86d2-eae585442104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75b7b0a4-c7ee-525c-b9ca-1a7df7652ed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-110343" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x},\\, & x>0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=-\\sqrt[3]{X},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=-\\sqrt[3]{X}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x>0,$ we have $y<0.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2b1503e6624c9060829468c9fa0d49856a2d76131975b6b1e437b7de3285c7.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y<0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\sqrt[3]{X}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-X\\le {y}^{3}) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge -{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(-{y}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-3{y}^{2})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-{y}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{-4(-{y}^{3})} \\\\  & =12{y}^{2}{e}^{4{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{y}^{2}{e}^{4{y}^{3}},\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09920ab5-927e-5144-b904-cafb0b14ca5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}(1-9{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551686} {:answer/id #uuid "202d7c13-d634-5a77-af5c-845389655864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}(9-{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551735} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7990c6-c565-5878-9b66-56ed047222ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(1-9{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551775} {:answer/id #uuid "a839825b-9be8-5988-b3e5-e0aa662c915b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{8}(1-3{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552068} {:answer/id #uuid "b55a125e-dbc5-56ad-bd31-db73ae35ac74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}(9-{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09920ab5-927e-5144-b904-cafb0b14ca5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}(1-9{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551686} :answer-field/id #uuid "85d2285e-2456-56af-a314-257530350a9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7be864d2-62c5-5172-87fe-bf187923ab8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-151981" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{x}^{2}),\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{X}{3},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ in terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=\\frac{X}{3}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $0<y<\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n![](images/42/42a3c2510927b66842ec0360eabfb169a306ccaf52292132f31769036e608e69.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{X}{3}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 3y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{3},$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-(3y)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(1-9{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}(1-9{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2008912a-15de-5248-a9b5-c375eb3dc292" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the PDF of a Random Variable Under an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7bf6b1-d530-59ea-9dc8-7b99db3d0094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y}{3}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551692} {:answer/id #uuid "165a73fa-f469-5a44-86ef-009948eb5611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}, & \\,1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551713} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc1534a-6a01-51a4-9c6e-4184dd7e0bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551733} {:answer/id #uuid "b5dc456d-f743-5286-851a-c035feeffac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552105} {:answer/id #uuid "d167a7a8-34d5-59c3-b949-f001717e3bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5dc456d-f743-5286-851a-c035feeffac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552105} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f2e9ff-fa41-50ab-a63c-2d1d1f490067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8460a557-83b6-5efd-a8a5-c94e63881b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-151985" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3},\\, & 1<x<2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ in terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $1<x<2,$ we have $2<y<4.$\n\n![](images/60/60342277a025cb26cebab2ac534fad4aa1b49fc2285571c303c4e829eb464606.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $2<y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $2<y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2(\\frac{y}{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
